BT /F1 8 Tf 42 748 Td 10.2 TL
(      ) Tj
(10452 rows, so only the cost changed.) Tj
T*
(  ### Results) Tj
T*
(  | index | best | EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN |) Tj
T*
(  |---|---:|---|) Tj
T*
(  | none | 397.97 ms | SCAN readings |) Tj
T*
(  | \(sensor_id, ts, variable\) | 0.19 ms | SEARCH readings USING COVERING INDEX ux_readings \(sensor_id=? AND) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(ts>? AND ts<?\) |) Tj
T*
(  ### Discussion) Tj
T*
(  SCAN readings means SQLite reads every row in the table and keeps the ones that match, so a question about) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(one mote on one day costs a walk over all 9119212 rows. SEARCH means it uses the index instead, a) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(second copy of \(sensor_id, ts, variable\) kept in sorted order, so it can jump straight to that sensor) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(and then to that day inside it. It reads about 10452 entries rather than the whole table, which is the) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(difference between the two timings.) Tj
T*
(  The part in parentheses says which conditions the index actually handled, and here it handled all three.) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(That depends on column order: sensor_id comes first, so one mote's readings sit together and its day is) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(a contiguous run inside them. An index built as \(ts, sensor_id\) would constrain only ts and read that) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(day for all 54 motes, thirty-seven times as many rows. COVERING means every column the query needs is) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(already in the index, so the table is never opened.) Tj
T*
(  ) Tj
T*
(  ## Part 4 The Three-Way Comparison) Tj
T*
(  ### Method) Tj
T*
(  All three engines answer the same question, the average temperature of each of the 53 motes that reported) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(one. Only the query itself is timed, warm, best of 5. Lines of code counts the expression that produces) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(the answer rather than the setup, so the pandas read_parquet call is not included.) Tj
T*
(  ### Results) Tj
T*
(  | engine | before it can answer | query | lines of code |) Tj
T*
(  |---|---|---:|---:|) Tj
T*
(  | pandas, Parquet into RAM | 0.5 s read | 105.5 ms | 1 |) Tj
T*
(  | SQLite, lab.db | 22.2 s load | 1019.9 ms | 2 |) Tj
T*
(  | DuckDB, Parquet | none | 19.0 ms | 2 |) Tj
T*
(  ### Discussion) Tj
T*
(  pandas and SQLite return exactly the same numbers, and DuckDB differs from them by at most 1.33e-12. That) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(is a different order of addition rather than a different answer: pandas and SQLite accumulate row by) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(row, DuckDB adds partial sums from vectorised chunks, and floating-point addition is not associative.) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(DuckDB is also the fastest and needs nothing prepared, since it reads the Parquet tree in place, while) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(SQLite is slowest because the long schema makes it walk the whole table.) Tj
T*
(  ) Tj
T*
(  ## Part 5 When You Would Choose Each Store) Tj
T*
(  A row store keeps one reading's fields next to each other on disk, and it reads in fixed-size pages, so a) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(page holds whole rows. Averaging the value column means loading pages that carry every other column) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(too, because value was never stored on its own. A column store writes all the values of one column) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(together, so reading value reads only value. The Parquet tree is 95 MB against lab.db's 1038 MB.) Tj
T*
(  SQLite is good at touching a small part of the data. Reading one sensor over one day takes a fraction of a) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(millisecond because the index leads straight to those rows, and correcting a single reading is one) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(UPDATE where the same fix in Parquet means rewriting the whole day's file. The file is also the data,) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(so a value written a second ago is already in the next query. And it is the only one of the two that) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(checks anything: the foreign key, STRICT and the CHECK reject a bad mote, a wrong type or an unknown) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(channel at insert time.) Tj
T*
(  I would keep the data in SQLite while it is still arriving or being corrected, and whenever a question) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(names one mote or one day. I would go to Parquet and DuckDB once the data is finished and the questions) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(change shape, scanning months but naming two or three columns. The deciding factor is the access) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(pattern rather than the size of the data, and since the export takes 0.9 s there is no reason to pick) Tj
T*
(      ) Tj
(only one.) Tj
T*
(  ) Tj
